# GI06 — Explain v3 ("Learn" depth): acute-on-chronic liver failure — definitions (EASL-CLIF, APASL, NACSELD, COSSH), scores and grades (AARC, CLIF-OF, CLIF-C ACLF),
# mortality by grade, infection and inflammation, organ support (infection, nutrition, coagulation, kidney and HRS-AKI, hepatopulmonary vs portopulmonary, lungs and circulation),
# futility, plasma exchange, transplant contraindications and post-transplant risk scores, and a true/false review.
# No mnemonics or hooks. Driven by the real questions in qpack/GI06.md (ACLF-16 hepatopulmonary vs portopulmonary asked 9 times; ACLF-13 coagulation and ACLF-15 HRS-AKI 3 each;
# ACLF-11 and ACLF-14 2 each; ACLF-02, ACLF-10 and ACLF-17 once; the other 14 cards never asked). Card-layer lines from gi_cardlayer.json are taught and agreed with.
# Fields as TOX01, plus SPINE, PRIMER and step. Table cards (no lettered parts) take parts a, b, c… in the order of the card’s rows or topics.
# Figures checked online on 20 Nov 2026:
#  - EASL CPG on ACLF (J Hepatol 2023), read in two parts: ≥4 organ failures or CLIF-C ACLF >70 after 3–7 days of full support with no transplant option → consider withdrawal (LoE 4, strong);
#    bacterial infection in about one third of ACLF admissions (up to 44% of AD patients who later develop ACLF), no precipitant found in about 35%; routine artificial/bioartificial support or
#    plasma exchange not recommended outside trials (LoE 2, strong); network meta-analysis TPE 3-month survival RR 0.74; noradrenaline first, MAP >65, stress steroids may be considered above
#    0.25 µg/kg/min, terlipressin or vasopressin second line; energy 30–35 kcal/kg/day (1–1.4 × REE), protein 1.2–1.5 g/kg (up to 2 g/kg critically ill), no protein restriction; IV glucose 2–3 g/kg/day
#    if fasting >12 h; two negative β-D-glucan tests allow antifungals to stop; invasive fungal infection a major contraindication to transplant; allocation systems underestimate waitlist mortality
#    in ACLF-2/3; ACLF-3 waitlist 14-day mortality 28%; transplant futility markers (Artru: active GI bleed, sepsis controlled <24 h, noradrenaline >50 µg/min, P/F <150; expert consensus: CFS
#    about 7 or more, ongoing sepsis, prior pan-drug-resistant infection, P/F <150, noradrenaline >1 µg/kg/min, lactate >9; lactate >4.4 at transplant independently predictive); TAM items
#    (age ≥53, lactate ≥4, ventilated with P/F <200, leucocytes ≤10 G/L), TAM 2 or more → 1-year survival 8.3%/10%; early transplant of ACLF-2/3 6-month survival 80.9% vs 10%.
#  - CANONIC (Moreau, Gastroenterology 2013, via MDedge summary): 1,343 patients; 28-day mortality no ACLF 4.7%, grade 1 22.1%, grade 2 32.0%, grade 3 76.7%; 90-day 14%, 40.7%, (52.3%), 79.1%.
#  - AARC (Choudhury, Hepatol Int 2017): five variables, score 5–15, grades 5–7 / 8–10 / 11–15, 28-day mortality 12.7% / 44.5% / 85.9%, AUROC 0.80 derivation, better than MELD and CLIF-SOFA.
#  - COSSH (Wu, Gut 2018): 1,322 patients; score 0.741×INR + 0.523×HBV-SOFA + 0.026×age + 0.003×bilirubin.
#  - NACSELD (Bajaj, Hepatology 2014): 507 infected cirrhotics; four failures (shock, HE III–IV, dialysis, ventilation); 30-day survival 92% / 72.6% / 51.3% / 36% / 23% for 0–4 failures.
#  - Kyoto APASL consensus (Hepatol Int, Feb 2025): proposed redefinition with bilirubin >5 and INR ≥1.5, ascites/HE within 28 days, kidney dysfunction (creatinine >1.5) optional; APASL excludes prior decompensation.
#  - Global multi-society ACLF definition (AASLD, EASL, APASL, ALEH, SOLDA; 30 Oct 2026, HCPLive report): liver failure (bilirubin ≥5, INR ≥1.5) required in advanced chronic liver disease plus
#    ≥1 extrahepatic organ dysfunction/failure; type A (no prior decompensation) and type B; kidney failure KDIGO stage 2–3 or RRT; brain West Haven 3–4; circulation vasopressors; lungs ventilation,
#    P/F ≤225 or S/F ≤244; grade 1 liver failure + one dysfunction, grade 2 + one failure, grade 3 + two or more failures; no steroids in grade 3; transplant only if 1-year survival expected >75–80%.
#  - ICA-ADQI 2024 HRS consensus (Nadim, J Hepatol 2024): HRS-AKI = creatinine ≥0.3 mg/dL in 48 h or ≥50% in 7 days and/or urine ≤0.5 mL/kg/h ≥6 h, no response within 24 h of adequate volume;
#    systematic 48-h albumin challenge no longer required; HRS-AKD <90 days, HRS-CKD >90 days; albumin 20–40 g/day; terlipressin infusion 2–12 mg/day (preferred) or 1–2 mg bolus 6-hourly;
#    up-titrate if creatinine not down 25%; stop at return to within 0.3 of baseline, or if no improvement after 48 h at maximum tolerated dose; maximum 14 days.
#  - Cavallin (Hepatology 2016): infusion vs bolus, response 76% vs 65% (NS), adverse events 35% vs 62%, lower effective dose with infusion.
#  - AGA 2021 coagulation in cirrhosis: no routine prophylactic platelets/FFP before common GI procedures; no routine TPO-RA; no recommendation on pre-procedure viscoelastic testing; standard VTE prophylaxis.
#  - StatPearls (HPS): A–a ≥15 (≥20 over 64); severity mild ≥80, moderate 60–<80, severe 50–<60, very severe <50; orthodeoxia = PaO2 fall >5% or >4 mmHg upright; bubbles 3rd–6th cycle.
#  - PORTICO (Sitbon, Lancet Gastroenterol Hepatol 2019): 85 patients, macitentan 10 mg, PVR 35% lower than placebo at week 12.
#  - SALT-M (J Hepatol 2023): age >50, one or ≥2 inotropes, respiratory failure, diabetes, BMI; c-statistic 0.72 / 0.80.
# Second, independent review (20 Nov 2026) reopened: Kyoto consensus full text (Hepatol Int 2025;19:1–69, Springer; proposed redefinition with ‘with or without kidney dysfunction (serum creatinine
#   >1.5 mg/dl)’, AKI staging preferred to absolute creatinine); AASLD press release 30 Oct 2026 (Hepatology, doi 10.1097/HEP.0000000000001891) and HCPLive for the global definition; AARC abstract
#   (Choudhury 2017: score ≥11 at baseline or persisting in week 1 among non-survivors) and an AARC calculator for the cut-offs; CANONIC abstract (no-ACLF 28-day mortality 1.9%); EF-CLIF report of
#   APACHE (EASL Congress 2026: 274 patients, mostly ACLF-1b/2, death without transplant at 90 days 37.8% vs 49.6%); Muciño-Bermejo (Ann Hepatol 2012) and Garcia-Tsao (Hepatology 2008) on AKI causes;
#   Bridging review (Univ. Barcelona repository) on transplant ventilation limits (FiO2 under 50%, P/F above 150–200) and contraindications (noradrenaline >1 µg/kg/min, lactate >9, CFS ≥7).
# Still not traced to a primary source (worded softly or flagged): the 89% one-year ventilated mortality on ACLF-17, the plasma exchange survival figures on ACLF-19, ‘NACSELD best for futility’
# on ACLF-09 (no guideline says so), the 90-day no-ACLF figure on ACLF-08.

SPINE = [
  ("Chronic liver, acute hit, organs fail", "ACLF is an acute insult on chronic liver disease that tips the patient into organ failure and a short-term mortality far above simple decompensation, and the schools differ mainly on which patients and which insults they let in."),
  ("Inflammation and infection drive it", "A cirrhotic immune system that is both paralysed and over-inflamed, fed by bacteria translocating from the gut, makes infection the commonest trigger and the first thing to treat."),
  ("Counting failed organs sets the grade", "The EASL-CLIF system counts failed organs with the CLIF-OF score, treats the kidney as the organ that fails earliest, and the grade it gives predicts 28-day death better than MELD."),
  ("Kidney, clotting and nutrition support", "Rebalanced haemostasis means the INR does not predict bleeding, kidney injury is usually volume or tubular and only sometimes HRS, and HRS-AKI is treated with terlipressin and albumin while transplant is arranged."),
  ("Lungs and circulation", "The cirrhotic circulation is vasodilated and hyperdynamic, so shock gets noradrenaline and albumin, and hypoxaemia must be sorted into pneumonia, fluid, hydrothorax, hepatopulmonary syndrome or portopulmonary hypertension."),
  ("Futility, rescue and transplant", "Full support is a time-limited trial reassessed at 3–7 days, transplant is the only definitive treatment, and the scores at the end of the deck say who is too sick to survive it."),
]

PRIMER = "Cirrhosis has a compensated phase, when the patient feels well, and a decompensated phase marked by ascites, encephalopathy, variceal bleeding or jaundice; most decompensations are admitted, treated and survived. ACLF is the minority of those admissions in which organs outside the liver fail as well, and it carries a 28-day mortality of a fifth to three quarters depending on how many organs fail. The cirrhotic circulation is already vasodilated, with a low systemic vascular resistance and a high cardiac output, and the cirrhotic immune system is both overactive and ineffective; these two facts explain most of the deck. MELD (bilirubin, INR, creatinine, with sodium in MELD-Na) ranks patients for transplant but was built for chronic disease and misses extrahepatic organ failure."

